<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Control maps and reliable checks · Umicom</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css">@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a><a href="../products.html">Applications</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>Control maps and reliable checks</h1>
<p>A useful GUI test has two responsibilities: locate the intended control, and check the result of the requested operation. A control map supports the first responsibility. Explicit assertions support the second.</p>
<p>Consider an Umicom Notes window with Save, Archive and a text editor. The Save and Archive buttons need distinct identifiers. The text input inside the editor can use its outer component's identifier. After requesting Save, a separate file check verifies the saved bytes. These are related pieces of one test, not interchangeable signs of success.</p>
<h2>Where the code lives</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Purpose and example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/control_inventory.h</code></td><td>The copied control-record and summary contracts. The complete example below creates six Notes records.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>src/ui/control_inventory.c</code></td><td>Validates parents, window scopes, IDs and state flags; retains rows and calculates target coverage.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>src/ui/interaction_recording.c</code></td><td>Exports the map through the existing escaped JSON/HTML writer used by interaction recordings.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/automation.h</code> and <code>src/ui/automation.c</code></td><td>Scenarios, assertion validation, report storage and acceptance checks. The Notes save example demonstrates reading a file after an action.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/gtk4/interaction_recording.h</code></td><td>The public GTK capture and privacy boundary. Use it from the window's owner thread.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/interaction_recording_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Captures live GTK metadata and connects Inspect controls to the existing Report problem panel.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/automation_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Resolves targets, performs toolkit actions and observes entries, editors, buttons and other supported widgets.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tests/gui_control_checks/</code></td><td>Core boundary cases and native fixtures for missing, delayed, disabled, private and ambiguous controls.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>examples/gui_recording/control_checks.c</code></td><td>The executable control-map example. Its negative mode deliberately creates a repeated Save identifier.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Umicom::ui</code> and <code>Umicom::ui_gtk4</code></td><td>Link the core example to the first target and a GTK host to the second. Applications do not need their own inventory implementation.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Understand one record</h2>
<p><code>UmiUiControlRecord</code> describes metadata, not a widget. <code>parentIndex</code> points to an earlier row. A root uses <code>UMI_UI_CONTROL_NO_PARENT</code>. <code>scopeId</code> identifies one captured window; its children use the same value. Each root begins a new scope. The numbers are local to the map and must not be saved as permanent widget identities.</p>
<p><code>automationId</code> is the stable test name assigned by the application component. <code>roleName</code> describes the control type rather than its visible caption. The flags are integers with only two valid values: 0 for false and 1 for true. A mapped control must also be effectively visible. A disabled ancestor makes a child effectively disabled even when the child's own enabled property has not changed.</p>
<p>A private subtree becomes one record whose role is <code>private-control</code>. Its identifier is empty and no children are captured. This uses the existing recording privacy marker and password recognition. The map deliberately has no text-value field.</p>
<h2>Use the core contracts</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>How to call it</th><th>Ownership and failure</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryCreate</code></td><td>Choose a capacity between 1 and 4,096; pass an output pointer. The example requests six rows.</td><td>The model owns its allocated array. A failed create clears the output pointer.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryAdd</code></td><td>Add parents before children. An empty direct ID is allowed; repeated IDs are retained for inspection.</td><td>The record is copied. Invalid fields, a full array or an invalid parent leave earlier records unchanged.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryCount</code></td><td>Read the number of stored rows before iterating.</td><td>No pointer to internal row storage is returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryAt</code></td><td>Copy a zero-based row into caller-owned storage.</td><td>An out-of-range index returns Not Found. Read the output only after success.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryTarget</code></td><td>Find a row's own ID or its nearest tagged ancestor.</td><td>Returns a row index, not a widget. Private or unaddressable rows return Not Found and the sentinel output.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventorySummarise</code></td><td>Count tagged, inherited, unaddressable and repeated targets.</td><td>These counts describe testability. They are not feature or callback pass counts.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryWriteJson</code> / <code>WriteHtml</code></td><td>Provide a <code>UmiUiRecordingWriteFn</code> callback and its context, as the example does with stdout.</td><td>The writer borrows the sink; the caller checks final flushing and closes it. A sink failure stops output.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UmiUiControlInventoryDestroy</code></td><td>Release the model after inspection or export.</td><td>Passing NULL is harmless. Copied output records remain caller-owned.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The inventory has one owner thread. Do not modify or destroy it inside its export callback. Parent indices only point backwards, so a retained map cannot contain a parent cycle. The core performs no filesystem, database, network or GTK operations.</p>
<h2>Build a Notes control map</h2>
<p>This command-line example describes a small Notes interface. It does not construct a graphical window. That makes the parent/child contract easy to inspect before using the native capture adapter. The private marker contains no password. The editor input inherits its ID from the editor component rather than inventing another independent test target.</p>
<pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_CONTROL_CHECKS_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<p>Build it from a configured Applications checkout:</p><pre><code>cmake --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-control-map-example
.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-control-map-example.exe
.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-control-map-example.exe --duplicate
$LASTEXITCODE</code></pre>
<p>The normal run reports six controls, one inherited target and zero ambiguous target records. It returns 0. The duplicate mode gives Archive the Save ID, counts the two conflicting records and returns 1. Input or export errors return 2. Add <code>--html</code> to produce the readable report; otherwise stdout contains JSON. The short summary goes to stderr so it does not corrupt the exported JSON.</p>
<p>On Linux, use <code>./build/headless-debug/bin/umicom-control-map-example</code> after building the same target. The example's directory is also an independent installed-SDK project: configure it with <code>CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH</code> pointing to the installed Framework. Its existing CMake file uses <code>find_package(UmicomFramework CONFIG REQUIRED)</code>.</p>
<h2>Capture real controls</h2>
<p>A GTK host uses <code>UmiGtk4ControlInventoryCapture</code> rather than describing the rows itself. Pass its existing <code>GtkWindow</code>, a bounded capacity and an output pointer on the GTK owner thread. The capture visits that window and related transient dialogs, skips the recorder window, and returns a copied model. It does not invoke the controls or retain their widget pointers.</p>
<pre><code>#include "umicom/ui/gtk4/interaction_recording.h"

/* Inside a GTK owner callback; window is the application's GtkWindow. */
UmiUiControlInventory *map = NULL;
UmiStatus status = UmiGtk4ControlInventoryCapture(window, 4096U, &amp;map);
if (status == UMI_STATUS_OK) {
    UmiUiControlInventorySummary summary;
    status = UmiUiControlInventorySummarise(map, &amp;summary);
    /* Present the copied summary through the host's status component. */
}
UmiUiControlInventoryDestroy(map);</code></pre>
<p>The fragment belongs inside an existing window callback; the complete command-line example above supplies an independent buildable programme. <code>UmiGtk4RecordingIsPrivate</code> checks a component and its parents. Use the existing <code>UmiGtk4RecordingSetPrivate</code> for custom private views. Screenshots continue to use their separate explicit-consent path.</p>
<p>An oversized tree or invalid record causes capture to discard its candidate and return an error with a NULL output. A failed map is not published with its remaining rows silently omitted. Traversal is bounded to 256 levels; the number of retained rows is bounded by the capacity. No loop processes pending GUI events during this snapshot.</p>
<h2>Add checks to an automation scenario</h2>
<p>The existing <code>UmiUiAutomationStep</code> describes a target and operation. The four state assertions <code>ASSERT_VISIBLE</code>, <code>ASSERT_ENABLED</code>, <code>ASSERT_FOCUSED</code> and <code>ASSERT_SELECTED</code> use the exact strings <code>"true"</code> and <code>"false"</code> in <code>value</code>. The full names begin with <code>UMI_UI_AUTOMATION_</code>.</p>
<p><code>UmiUiAutomationStepValidate</code> checks IDs, operation values and terminated UTF-8 before dispatch. <code>UmiUiAutomationObservationCheck</code> compares a successful driver's assertion or wait result with the returned observation, including its target ID. If the driver says OK but returns different text, a false required flag or malformed observation fields, the runner records a failed step. Ordinary action results still need a separate outcome assertion.</p>
<p>Existing custom assertion drivers must return the state they actually inspected. For example, after reading a Notes file, copy its actual text into <code>observation.text</code>, put the requested target ID in <code>observation.target_id</code>, and place the expected text in <code>step.value</code>. Do not copy the expected value into the observation merely to make it match. The existing <a href="gui-recording.html">Notes save example</a> shows the complete file read and retains its independent comparison.</p>
<p><code>umi_ui_automation_run</code> still returns OK when it successfully creates a report containing product failures. Call <code>UmiUiAutomationReportRequireSuccess(report, expectedStepCount)</code> to require a nonempty, complete, failure-free run. The report owns copied results until destroyed. Recording imports continue to omit expected values, observed text and free-form driver messages.</p>
<h2>GTK action behaviour</h2>
<p>The driver resolves a stable ID within its root, optional observed scope and related window family. More than one matching widget is an error: it does not click the first one. An unparented fixture cannot search other windows in the process. If identical IDs exist in a parent and a modal dialog, use a driver rooted in the dialog when that is the intended target.</p>
<p>WAIT operations keep looking until the target is available or the deadline expires. This includes controls created after the wait begins and controls replaced by a new widget with the same ID. The driver processes a bounded number of pending events before checking the deadline again. A single blocking callback can still stall the owner thread, so an external test timeout remains necessary.</p>
<p>Visibility requires effective ancestor visibility and GTK mapping. Enabled state includes ancestor sensitivity. Focus refers to the exact widget. Selected state means active for toggle/check/switch controls; for a drop-down it means that some row is selected, not that a particular row index was selected. Verify the actual index or domain state separately when it matters.</p>
<p>TYPE_TEXT supports GtkEditable entries and GtkTextView editors, refuses read-only or private targets, and assigns text through the widget API. Text assertions are bounded by the existing 511-byte payload. Larger documents need document-service assertions rather than a truncated text comparison. Password values are not returned in metadata observations.</p>
<p>Programmatic fixture actions remain usable before a window is presented. Consequently, important user journeys should first WAIT_VISIBLE and WAIT_ENABLED, then perform the action and check its result. This protects existing toolkit tests without pretending they simulate real pointer hit testing, keyboard input or desktop occlusion.</p>
<h2>Use the same components in future applications</h2>
<p>A training banking form and a paper-order panel can use the same map and assertion contracts. Each supplies its own stable IDs and fixtures; neither needs another recorder, driver report or inventory store. Keep durable defect histories behind the Data Server. A locally exported control map is diagnostic evidence, not a replacement database.</p>
<p>When an action fails, keep its starting state, scope, ID, expected outcome and observed result together. Inspect disabled states for legitimate prerequisites. Add a regression scenario for the reproduced problem, then rerun that scenario after the correction.</p>
<h2>Further reading</h2><p><a href="../GUI_CONTROL_CHECKS.html">Inspecting application controls</a> · <a href="gui-recording.html">Recordings and independent outcomes</a> · <a href="components.html">Framework component composition</a> · <a href="../source-map.html">Public-header source map</a></p>
<p>GTK's own documentation explains <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/method.Widget.is_sensitive.html">effective sensitivity</a>, <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/method.Widget.is_visible.html">ancestor visibility</a> and <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/method.Widget.activate.html">widget activation</a>.</p></main><footer>Umicom Foundation</footer></body></html>